    <!-- ===== PRACTICAL TIPS ===== -->
    <section id="tips">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-header">
          <h2 id="at-the-event-practical-hearing-protection">At the Event: Practical Hearing Protection</h2>
          <p class="section-sub">Earplugs only work if worn correctly. Attenuation data assumes a proper acoustic seal, a loosely inserted earplug may provide half the rated protection or less.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="hearing-tip-grid">
          <div class="hearing-tip">
            <span class="hearing-tip-icon">📍</span>
            <div>
              <strong style="color: var(--text);">Insert before entering the venue.</strong> Sound damage begins immediately at high SPLs. Don't wait until your ears start ringing, by then, temporary threshold shift has already occurred.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="hearing-tip">
            <span class="hearing-tip-icon">🔄</span>
            <div>
              <strong style="color: var(--text);">Seal check: insert, then tug lightly.</strong> For flanged silicone tips, a slight resistance indicates a proper seal. If they fall out easily, try a different tip size. An imperfect seal can reduce effective attenuation by 10+ dB.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="hearing-tip">
            <span class="hearing-tip-icon">📏</span>
            <div>
              <strong style="color: var(--text);">Position matters, distance halves SPL every doubling.</strong> Moving from 1m to 2m from a speaker reduces intensity by ~6 dB. Standing back from sub-woofers reduces low-frequency exposure significantly even before earplugs.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="hearing-tip">
            <span class="hearing-tip-icon">⏸</span>
            <div>
              <strong style="color: var(--text);">Take acoustic breaks.</strong> 10–15 minutes in a quiet space per hour of loud exposure reduces cumulative dose. The chill-out room, smoking area, or outdoor space are not just social, they're recovery time for your cochlea.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="hearing-tip">
            <span class="hearing-tip-icon">🧼</span>
            <div>
              <strong style="color: var(--text);">Clean and store reusables properly.</strong> Silicone earplugs accumulate earwax which can compromise the acoustic filter. Rinse with water after each use and allow to air-dry. Most last 6–12 months of regular use before replacement.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="hearing-tip">
            <span class="hearing-tip-icon">🎧</span>
            <div>
              <strong style="color: var(--text);">Watch your personal listening volume too.</strong> WHO recommends keeping personal audio device volume below 80 dB for no more than 40 hours per week. Give your cochlea recovery time between events, avoid loud headphone use the day after a show.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Tinnitus Alert -->
        <div class="tinnitus-alert">
          <h3 id="know-the-warning-signs-of-hearing-damage">Know the Warning Signs of Hearing Damage</h3>
          <p>See an audiologist if you experience any of the following, especially if persistent after 48 hours:</p>
          <div class="tinnitus-symptoms">
            <span>Ringing or buzzing (tinnitus)</span>
            <span>Muffled or "underwater" hearing</span>
            <span>Difficulty understanding speech in noise</span>
            <span>Fullness or pressure in the ear</span>
            <span>Sounds seem distorted or tinny</span>
            <span>Increased sensitivity to everyday sounds</span>
          </div>
          <p style="margin-top: 16px; font-size: 0.88rem;">Temporary tinnitus and threshold shift after a show are signs your cochlear hair cells were stressed. Repeated exposures compound permanent damage. Persistent tinnitus (lasting more than 48 hours) warrants urgent audiological evaluation, some interventions are most effective within the first 72 hours of onset.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

